WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:03.460
In the Star Wars universe, dead doesn't always
mean dead.

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:07.800
Thanks to the series' complicated canon, there
are plenty of ways that this lightning-slinging

00:00:07.819 --> 00:00:12.260
villain could have come back from the grave
- or, possibly, escaped death entirely.

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:15.200
Here's how we know Palpatine is coming back
for Episode 9.

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:19.000
"Soon I'll be dead — and you with me."

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:26.460
In Star Wars, just because you fell into a
seemingly bottomless pit doesn't mean that

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:27.000
you died.

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:31.060
Luke jumped off of a platform in Cloud City,
and despite the fact that he only had one

00:00:31.069 --> 00:00:32.579
hand, ended up just fine.

00:00:32.579 --> 00:00:36.920
In the Legends timeline, also known as the
old Expanded Universe, Boba Fett climbed out

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:40.000
of the Sarlacc pit and went on to cause even
more trouble.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.430
According to Samuel L. Jzackson, even Mace
Windu might still be alive.

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:44.140
"I'm not dead.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:48.940
Jedis can fall incredibly high distances and
not die."

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:52.899
According to Jackson, George Lucas has even
co-signed that theory.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:54.579
Heck, look at what happened with Darth Maul.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:58.780
At the end of The Phantom Menace, Obi-Wan
Kenobi cut Maul in half and then sent him

00:00:58.789 --> 00:01:00.609
careening down a reactor shaft.

00:01:00.609 --> 00:01:02.060
Somehow, it didn't stick.

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:06.720
Despite missing half of his body, Maul rescued
himself using the Force, got himself a set

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:10.580
of mechanical legs, and went on to become
a major thorn in the galaxy's side.

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:15.240
In The Clone Wars, Maul united the galaxy's
underworld and took over the planet Mandalore.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:19.600
He then went on to make an appearance in Solo
as the head of the Crimson Dawn crime syndicate.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:23.440
And he'd probably still be kicking around
in the sequel trilogy if he hadn't challenged

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:26.100
Obi-Wan Kenobi to an ill-advised rematch in
Rebels.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:31.040
Characters like Maul and Boba Fett went on
to have robust story arcs after their on-screen

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:34.220
deaths, even if their ends looked pretty decisive
at the time.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:40.020
We're not even mentioning Yoda, Obi-Wan, Qui-Gon,
Anakin, and all the other Jedi who came back

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:40.760
as ghosts.

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:44.930
If you don't think the Emperor is a big enough
deal to warrant that kind of second life treatment,

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:45.600
think again.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:49.920
"You will find that it is you who are mistaken."

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:55.120
In Revenge of the Sith, Palpatine finally
exposes himself as a Sith lord and tries to

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:56.720
sway Anakin away from the light.

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:01.980
He does so by making a promise, claiming that
the dark side can grant talented Sith immortality,

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:04.180
and even bring people back from the dead.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:09.070
As Palpatine explains, a Sith Lord known as
Darth Plagueis once figured out how to manipulate

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:12.770
midi-chlorians using the Force, unlocking
the secrets of life itself.

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:17.190
Using his powers, Plagueis was able to keep
his loved ones alive, supernaturally prolong

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:21.050
his own life, and even create new living beings
from scratch like Anakin.

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:29.280
"The dark side of the force is a pathway to
many abilities some consider to be unnatural."

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:34.160
Unfortunately, Plagueis made one mistake:
he taught his apprentice everything he knew.

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:38.290
Once Plagueis' second-in-command learned all
of his master's secrets, he killed Plagueis

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:41.300
while the elder Sith was sleeping, becoming
the new Dark Lord.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:42.680
And who was that apprentice?

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:43.940
Why, Palpatine, of course.

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:47.990
We know from Revenge of the Sith and some
Star Wars comics that he knew all of Plagueis'

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:50.340
tricks, including creating new life.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:51.180
"There was no father.."

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:55.490
Since we've already seen Palpatine use his
powers to create new life, then it follows

00:02:55.490 --> 00:02:56.880
that he'd have the power to cheat death.

00:02:57.100 --> 00:03:00.590
If Palpatine used his knowledge to escape
death at the hands of his own apprentice,

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:02.380
it wouldn't be too much of a stretch.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:07.040
You remember how Maul and Boba Fett came back
to life in the old Expanded Universe?

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:08.080
Palpatine did that too.

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:12.780
In Dark Empire, a series published by Dark
Horse Comics in the early '90s, the Emperor

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:17.480
returns about five years after Return of the
Jedi by transferring his consciousness into

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:18.420
a clone body.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:23.440
In fact, as Luke Skywalker comes to learn,
Palpatine created an entire stockpile of clones

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:25.240
that he can jump between as he sees fit.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:30.040
That makes destroying Palpatine near impossible
- if you kill one clone, he'll just use another

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:34.330
one - which turns out to be enough to make
a despairing Luke submit to the dark side.

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:39.480
When Disney bought Lucasfilm in 2012, it wiped
the slate clean for the Expanded Universe,

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:40.710
Palpatine's resurrection included.

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:45.180
But the new Star Wars canon hasn't shied away
from incorporating old Legends material when

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:46.000
it makes sense.

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:49.659
And of course, Star Wars is already lousy
with clones, meaning it wouldn't take a lot

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:50.920
to get audiences to buy in.

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:52.520
Would it be cool?

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:53.520
Well, maybe.

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:54.660
But is it possible?

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:55.820
You better believe it.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:00.020
"An old friend has learned the path to immortality."

